; ======================================================================
;  pl011_console.pi3 - the CONSOLE NAME SET the shared Anvil core prints
;                      and reads through, over the Pi 3's PL011.
;
;  Anvil/Core/*.pbi never names a chip. It calls UartWrite, UartWriteStr,
;  UartDrain, UartRead, UartReadReady and the message brackets, and the
;  compiler's own Print/PrintN keywords resolve BY NAME to three more.
;  The Pi 4 supplies all of them from the bottom third of
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4; the Arduino UNO Q supplies them from
;  ArduinoQ/Board/qcon_q.unoq over UEFI's text protocols. This file is the
;  Pi 3's answer, and it is the file that makes `pmf>` possible here.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE PRINT ROUTER, AND WHY THREE OF THESE NAMES LOOK ARBITRARY
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Print and PrintN are COMPILER KEYWORDS on this target. The router
;  picks a formatter from the argument's TYPE and calls one of three
;  procedures BY NAME, lowercased: str_print_at, printdec, printnl. It
;  does NOT choose the transport - whichever file defines those three
;  owns where every Print in the program goes.
;
;  Renaming one does not produce a link error. It produces a front-end
;  message about a missing string-output file, which is a confusing thing
;  to be told about a file you are looking at. Do not rename them, and do
;  not add a second copy of them in another file while they are here: two
;  definitions is the RIGHT error - a program has one Print destination -
;  but it will point at the wrong file.
;
;  WHEN A SECOND DESTINATION APPEARS ON THIS BOARD (the HDMI console of
;  milestone 3), the three names stay here and the MIRROR hangs off
;  UartWrite, exactly as it does on the Pi 4. A console is a transport
;  plus its taps, not two transports.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT THIS FILE OWNS AND WHAT RaspberryPi3/Lib/uart.pbi OWNS
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THIS FILE IS NOT A DRIVER. It is the seam's names over one, and the
;  driver is the boot lane's - the same file the immutable loader and the
;  serial updater are built from, proven on silicon. Nothing in it is
;  restated here.
;
;    uart.pbi DRIVES THE PART: the GPIO 14/15 ALT0 mux, the old-style
;    GPPUD/GPPUDCLK pull sequence the BCM2837 uses and the BCM2711 does
;    not, the baud divisor from a firmware-supplied clock, the line
;    control and control registers, and the bounded per-byte transmit and
;    receive. Pi3UartInit, Pi3UartWrite, Pi3UartRead and Pi3UartWaitClear
;    are the whole of what this file calls.
;
;    THIS FILE OWNS THE SEAM: the message bracket, the drop counter, the
;    three names the compiler's print router resolves, and the mapping
;    from the driver's return values onto the contracts THE CONSOLE BYTES
;    declares in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi.
;
;  THIS FILE DOES NOT TOUCH REGISTERS. RaspberryPi3/Lib/uart.pbi exposes
;  Pi3UartReadReady() beside the consuming Pi3UartRead(), so the seam can
;  report readiness without duplicating the PL011 register knowledge.
;
;  WHAT THE SEAM REQUIRES AND THE DRIVER ALREADY HAS, checked rather than
;  assumed: BOUNDED WRITES - Pi3UartWrite waits on the transmit-full flag
;  through Pi3UartWaitClear, which is bounded by the system timer and by
;  an iteration count - and DRAIN ON THE LAST STOP BIT rather than on an
;  empty queue, which is exactly what Pi3UartWaitClear(8) waits for.
;  Neither needed a change.
;
;  A CONSEQUENCE WORTH KNOWING: this file works only after Pi3UartInit()
;  has run. Before that the port is not muxed, the divisor is not set,
;  and a byte written here goes nowhere at all - silently, because there
;  is no bit that says "this port was never configured". UartRunning()
;  answers that by reading the driver's own ready flag rather than
;  keeping a second one.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  BOUNDED, NEVER WEDGED - except in the one place where blocking is the
;  whole point
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UartWrite() gives up after #P3C_SPIN_MAX attempts and counts the
;  character. A monitor that spins for ever on a FIFO that will never
;  drain is a monitor that looks identical, from the other end of the
;  cable, to a board that has crashed - and this board is on a shared
;  bench behind a fifteen-second deadman that would then reset it with no
;  explanation.
;
;  UartRead() blocks for ever ON PURPOSE. It is the line editor's read
;  and waiting for a human is its entire job. Inside a transfer loop it
;  is the wrong call and UartReadWait() is the right one, which is the
;  same split RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4 makes for the same reason.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DEPENDENCIES: RaspberryPi3/Lib/uart.pbi must be included BEFORE this
;  file (Pi3UartInit, Pi3UartWaitClear, pi3_uart_ready, #PI3_UART), and
;  uart.pbi in turn needs RaspberryPi3/Lib/timer.pbi. House rule 10: this
;  file includes nothing itself.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UartReadReady() - check the software ring, then ask the driver.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; The default iteration bound for UartReadWait(). A count and not a
; duration on purpose: the contract is "one byte or -1", and a transfer
; that dies halfway must leave a live prompt behind rather than a wedged
; board on a shared bench.
#P3C_SPIN_MAX = 2000000

; The message ring. 8 KiB holds a long boot's worth of background
; sentences between drains; the drain runs on every newline, so this is a
; backstop for a drain that could not run, not an expected path.
#P3C_MSG_BYTES = 8192
; The network console is a second reader of accepted UART output. Keep its
; tap bounded and independent from the display mirror, just as on Pi 4.
; It is disabled until NetConsoleStart() explicitly enables it.
#P3C_AUX_BYTES = 8192
; Bytes accepted from PL011 while a synchronous Wi-Fi phase owns the
; command thread are held here and consumed by the next ordinary ReadLine.
; One slot stays empty to distinguish full from empty. A full ring stops
; admission without consuming another byte from the hardware FIFO.
#P3C_DEFERRED_RX_BYTES = 1024

Global p3c_dropped.i           ; characters UartWrite gave up on
Global Dim p3c_deferredRx.a[#P3C_DEFERRED_RX_BYTES]
Global p3c_deferredRxHead.i
Global p3c_deferredRxTail.i
Global p3c_deferredRxLost.i

; ======================================================================
;  THE MESSAGE CONTEXT - output that is FOR the operator but is not the
;  answer to the command they typed.
; ======================================================================
;  A background service - a lease renewing, a radio coming back, a link
;  arming - must never print onto the command line. The command line
;  carries the prompt, the typed input and the output of the command that
;  was run, and nothing else.
;
;  THE DECISION IS MADE HERE, AT THE ONE BYTE EVERY CONSOLE SHARES, and
;  not sentence by sentence in thirty callers. Code running on behalf of
;  nobody at the keyboard brackets itself with UartMessageBegin and
;  UartMessageEnd, and every byte written inside goes into the ring
;  instead of to the wire. Anvil/Core/messages.pbi registers a drain and
;  moves them out as lines. The bracket NESTS: a service that calls
;  another service is one message context, not two.
;
;  NOBODY REGISTERED, NOTHING DIVERTED. A diagnostic that includes this
;  file and has no message log must not have its output vanish into a
;  ring nobody reads, so with no drain the bracket is counted and ignored
;  and every byte goes where it always went.
;
;  A FATAL REPORT IS NOT A MESSAGE. The exception reporter calls
;  UartMessageAbandon() first, so a fault taken inside a background
;  service is still printed.
; ======================================================================
Global Dim p3c_msg.a[#P3C_MSG_BYTES]
Global p3c_msgDepth.i
Global p3c_msgHead.i
Global p3c_msgTail.i
Global p3c_msgLost.i
Global p3c_msgBytes.i
Global *p3c_msgDrain
Global p3c_msgBusy.i

; Independent auxiliary output tap consumed by common NetConsoleFlush.
; The screen mirror and this ring have separate cursors, so neither steals
; bytes from the other. Overflow drops newest and is counted.
Global Dim uart_aux.a[#P3C_AUX_BYTES]
Global uart_auxOn.i
Global uart_auxHead.i
Global uart_auxTail.i
Global uart_auxLost.i
Global *uart_auxFlush
Global uart_auxBusy.i
Global uart_auxBulk.i

; Register the message log's drain, or 0 to stop diverting. The drain
; MUST NOT print: it runs inside UartWrite. It is re-entry guarded
; regardless, because "must not" is not a mechanism.
Procedure UartMessageSetDrain(*fn)
  *p3c_msgDrain = *fn
EndProcedure

Procedure UartMessageBegin()
  p3c_msgDepth = p3c_msgDepth + 1
EndProcedure

; Closing the OUTERMOST bracket hands whatever the context wrote to the
; drain, so a message that did not end in a newline is not left sitting
; in the ring until the next one arrives and joins onto it.
Procedure UartMessageEnd()
  If p3c_msgDepth <= 0
    p3c_msgDepth = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  p3c_msgDepth = p3c_msgDepth - 1
  If p3c_msgDepth = 0 And *p3c_msgDrain <> 0 And p3c_msgBusy = 0
    p3c_msgBusy = 1
    p3c_msgDrain()
    p3c_msgBusy = 0
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; Leave every bracket at once. The fatal-exception path only.
Procedure UartMessageAbandon()
  p3c_msgDepth = 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i UartMessageDepth()
  ProcedureReturn p3c_msgDepth
EndProcedure

; 1 while a byte written now would be diverted.
Procedure.i UartMessageActive()
  If p3c_msgDepth > 0 And *p3c_msgDrain <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i UartMessageGet()
  Define c.i
  If p3c_msgTail = p3c_msgHead
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  c = p3c_msg[p3c_msgTail] & $FF
  p3c_msgTail = p3c_msgTail + 1
  If p3c_msgTail >= #P3C_MSG_BYTES
    p3c_msgTail = 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn c
EndProcedure

Procedure.i UartMessageLost()
  ProcedureReturn p3c_msgLost
EndProcedure

Procedure.i UartMessageBytes()
  ProcedureReturn p3c_msgBytes
EndProcedure

; Overflow drops the NEWEST and counts it. Dropping the oldest would
; silently rewrite history the operator has not read yet.
Procedure p3c_MessagePut(c.i)
  Define nxt.i
  Define n.i
  p3c_msgBytes = p3c_msgBytes + 1
  nxt = p3c_msgHead + 1
  If nxt >= #P3C_MSG_BYTES
    nxt = 0
  EndIf
  If nxt = p3c_msgTail
    p3c_msgLost = p3c_msgLost + 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  p3c_msg[p3c_msgHead] = c & $FF
  p3c_msgHead = nxt
  n = p3c_msgHead - p3c_msgTail
  If n < 0
    n = n + #P3C_MSG_BYTES
  EndIf
  If p3c_msgBusy = 0
    If c = 10 Or n > (#P3C_MSG_BYTES - 2048)
      p3c_msgBusy = 1
      p3c_msgDrain()
      p3c_msgBusy = 0
    EndIf
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; Enable or disable the common netconsole output tap.
Procedure UartAuxMirror(on.i)
  uart_auxOn = on
  If on = 0
    uart_auxHead = 0
    uart_auxTail = 0
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; The flush callback is called only from accepted-output handling. It must
; not print; the busy guard also prevents accidental recursive flushing.
Procedure UartAuxSetFlush(*fn)
  *uart_auxFlush = *fn
EndProcedure

Procedure UartAuxBulk(on.i)
  If on <> 0
    uart_auxBulk = 1
  Else
    uart_auxBulk = 0
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure.i UartAuxFillHigh()
  Define n.i
  n = uart_auxHead - uart_auxTail
  If n < 0 : n = n + #P3C_AUX_BYTES : EndIf
  If n > (#P3C_AUX_BYTES - 2048) : ProcedureReturn 1 : EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i UartAuxOn()
  ProcedureReturn uart_auxOn
EndProcedure

Procedure.i UartAuxGet()
  Define c.i
  If uart_auxTail = uart_auxHead : ProcedureReturn -1 : EndIf
  c = uart_aux[uart_auxTail] & $FF
  uart_auxTail = uart_auxTail + 1
  If uart_auxTail >= #P3C_AUX_BYTES : uart_auxTail = 0 : EndIf
  ProcedureReturn c
EndProcedure

Procedure.i UartAuxLost()
  ProcedureReturn uart_auxLost
EndProcedure

Procedure uart_AuxPut(c.i)
  Define nxt.i
  nxt = uart_auxHead + 1
  If nxt >= #P3C_AUX_BYTES : nxt = 0 : EndIf
  If nxt = uart_auxTail
    uart_auxLost = uart_auxLost + 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  uart_aux[uart_auxHead] = c & $FF
  uart_auxHead = nxt
  If *uart_auxFlush <> 0 And uart_auxBusy = 0
    If (uart_auxBulk = 0 And c = 10) Or UartAuxFillHigh() <> 0
      uart_auxBusy = 1
      uart_auxFlush()
      uart_auxBusy = 0
    EndIf
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  Transmit and receive.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UartWrite(c) - one byte.
;
;  THE DRIVER DOES THE WAITING AND THE BOUNDING. Pi3UartWrite() waits for
;  the transmit FIFO to stop being full and gives up rather than spinning
;  for ever, and answers 1 or 0. All this adds is the message bracket in
;  front of it and the drop counter behind it, which are the seam's
;  business and not the part's.
;
;  A MESSAGE NEVER TOUCHES THE WIRE. One compare when no bracket is open,
;  which is every character the operator ever waits for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i UartWrite(c.i)
  If p3c_msgDepth > 0
    If *p3c_msgDrain <> 0
      p3c_MessagePut(c)
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If Pi3UartWrite(c & $FF) <> 0
    ; HDMI is a mirror of the accepted UART stream, never a second print
    ; destination. The adapter makes no UART calls, so this cannot recurse.
    Pi3ScreenMirrorByte(c & $FF)
    If uart_auxOn <> 0
      uart_AuxPut(c & $FF)
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ; The port was never brought up, or its FIFO never drained. Count it:
  ; this is the number that separates "printed nothing" from "printed and
  ; the port ate it".
  p3c_dropped = p3c_dropped + 1
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UartWriteStr(*s) - a NUL-terminated string.
;
;  NOTE ON THE STAR, and it is not a typo: in a procedure taking "*s" the
;  bare name inside the body IS the address, and PeekB does the
;  dereferencing. PeekB SIGN-EXTENDS, so a byte of 200 arrives as -56;
;  the NUL test would still work but the character would be wrong, so it
;  is masked.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure UartWriteStr(*s)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  i = 0
  Repeat
    c = PeekB(*s + i) & $FF
    If c = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    UartWrite(c)
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
EndProcedure

; CR and LF both, because the reader is a terminal.
Procedure UartWriteNl()
  UartWrite(13)
  UartWrite(10)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; UartReadReady() - software-deferred data first, then hardware readiness.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure UartReadReady()
  If p3c_deferredRxHead <> p3c_deferredRxTail
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn Pi3UartReadReady()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UartRead() - one byte. BLOCKS FOR EVER, deliberately.
;
;  The line editor's read; waiting for a human is the whole job. Inside a
;  transfer loop this is the wrong call - use UartReadWait(), which
;  leaves a live prompt behind when the wire dies.
;
;  Pi3UartRead() answers -1 for "nothing there" and -2 for a receive
;  error or a port that was never brought up. BOTH ARE SKIPPED HERE and
;  the difference matters: a framing error is a byte this console has no
;  honest value for, and handing the line editor a corrupted character
;  would put it in the operator's command line. The driver has already
;  cleared the error. A port that was never brought up loops here for
;  ever, which is correct - there is nothing to read and nothing to say
;  it on.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i UartRead()
  Define c.i
  Repeat
    If p3c_deferredRxHead <> p3c_deferredRxTail
      c = p3c_deferredRx[p3c_deferredRxHead]
      p3c_deferredRxHead = (p3c_deferredRxHead + 1) % #P3C_DEFERRED_RX_BYTES
    Else
      c = Pi3UartRead()
    EndIf
  Until c >= 0
  ProcedureReturn c
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UartReadWait(spins) - one byte, or -1 if none arrived.
;
;  spins is an iteration count; pass 0 for the default.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i UartReadWait(spins.i)
  Define n.i
  Define c.i
  If spins <= 0
    spins = #P3C_SPIN_MAX
  EndIf
  n = 0
  Repeat
    If p3c_deferredRxHead <> p3c_deferredRxTail
      c = p3c_deferredRx[p3c_deferredRxHead]
      p3c_deferredRxHead = (p3c_deferredRxHead + 1) % #P3C_DEFERRED_RX_BYTES
    Else
      c = Pi3UartRead()
    EndIf
    If c >= 0
      ProcedureReturn c
    EndIf
    n = n + 1
  Until n >= spins
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; Admit a small batch of physical input without invoking the line editor or
; command dispatcher. The currently running command is still on the stack;
; bytes remain ordered in this ring and are returned by UartRead after it
; completes. When full, leave additional bytes in PL011's hardware FIFO.
Procedure.i UartAdmitPending(maxBytes.i)
  Protected n.i, c.i, tailNext.i, attempts.i
  If maxBytes < 1 : ProcedureReturn 0 : EndIf
  If maxBytes > 32 : maxBytes = 32 : EndIf
  n = 0 : attempts = 0
  While attempts < maxBytes
    If Pi3UartReadReady() = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    tailNext = (p3c_deferredRxTail + 1) % #P3C_DEFERRED_RX_BYTES
    If tailNext = p3c_deferredRxHead
      Break
    EndIf
    c = Pi3UartRead()
    attempts = attempts + 1
    If c = -1 : Break : EndIf
    If c = -2
      p3c_deferredRxLost = p3c_deferredRxLost + 1
      Continue
    EndIf
    p3c_deferredRx[p3c_deferredRxTail] = c & $FF
    p3c_deferredRxTail = tailNext
    n = n + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

Procedure.i UartDeferredRxLost()
  ProcedureReturn p3c_deferredRxLost
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UartDrain() - block, bounded, until everything has left the wire.
;
;  BUSY, NOT "FIFO EMPTY", and the driver already has exactly that wait:
;  Pi3UartWaitClear(8) waits for the transmitter-busy flag, bounded by
;  the system timer and by an iteration count. An empty FIFO only means
;  the last byte reached the shift register; BUSY stays set until that
;  byte and all of its stop bits have actually been sent. The difference
;  is one character time, and it is the difference between a clean last
;  line and a truncated one when the next thing the monitor does is reset
;  the board - which here is what happens after `reboot` and after a
;  failed A/B confirm.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i UartDrain()
  ProcedureReturn Pi3UartWaitClear(8)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UartDropped() - how many characters UartWrite() has given up on.
;
;  Zero with no output means the port was never brought up or is not
;  routed; non-zero means it is there and not draining.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i UartDropped()
  ProcedureReturn p3c_dropped
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UartRunning() - has the port been brought up.
;
;  READ FROM uart.pbi's OWN FLAG rather than kept a second time here.
;  Two files each remembering whether the port is up is two answers that
;  can disagree, and the one that would be wrong is this one - uart.pbi
;  is what sets it, and uart.pbi is what would clear it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i UartRunning()
  ProcedureReturn pi3_uart_ready
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UartInit(baud) - the core's name for bringing the port up.
;
;  THE CLOCK COMES FROM THE FIRMWARE AND CANNOT BE BAKED. The PL011's
;  input clock on this part is whatever the firmware set, read through
;  the property mailbox as clock id 2; it is 48 MHz on a stock Pi 3 and
;  it is not a constant this file may assume. So this entry point is a
;  convenience for a caller that already has the number, and the monitor
;  itself calls Pi3ClockRate(2) and Pi3UartInit() directly during the
;  pre-MMU phase - see the ORDER section of RaspberryPi3/Lib/mmu.pi3 for
;  why every mailbox transaction has to happen there.
;
;  It answers 0 rather than guessing a clock. A console brought up at the
;  wrong divisor prints plausible-looking rubbish, which is worse than
;  printing nothing: the operator debugs their terminal.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i UartInit(baud.i)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i UartInitClk(baud.i, clkhz.i)
  ProcedureReturn Pi3UartInit(clkhz, baud)
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  THE PRINT ROUTER'S THREE PROCEDURES.
;
;  The compiler resolves these BY NAME, lowercased: str_print_at,
;  printdec, printnl. See the header before touching any of them.
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  str_print_at(p) - the raw-address string walk.
;
;  It takes a plain .i and not a "*s" for the reason the note in
;  UartWriteStr() gives: in a procedure taking "*s" the bare name IS the
;  address and "*s" dereferences it, so a plain .i is the clearer of the
;  two spellings here. The same choice, for the same reason, as the Pi 4
;  and RP2350 twins.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure str_print_at(p.i)
  Define c.i
  c = PeekB(p) & $FF
  While c <> 0
    UartWrite(c)
    p = p + 1
    c = PeekB(p) & $FF
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PrintDec(v) - a signed 64-bit integer in decimal.
;
;  THE MOST NEGATIVE VALUE IS THE TRAP AND IT IS HANDLED BY NOT NEGATING
;  IT. -9223372036854775808 has no positive counterpart in a signed
;  64-bit value, so `v = -v` on it returns the same number and a naive
;  loop prints nothing or spins. The digits are taken from the negative
;  side throughout and the sign is printed first, which needs no special
;  case at all.
;
;  A remainder taken from a negative dividend is negative here, so the
;  digit is 48 - r rather than 48 + r.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PrintDec(v.i)
  Define Dim d.a[24]
  Define n.i
  Define q.i
  Define r.i
  If v = 0
    UartWrite(48)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If v > 0
    v = -v
  Else
    UartWrite(45)
  EndIf
  n = 0
  While v <> 0
    q = v / 10
    r = v - q * 10
    d[n] = 48 - r
    n = n + 1
    v = q
  Wend
  While n > 0
    n = n - 1
    UartWrite(d[n])
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PrintNl() - end the line. CR and LF, because the reader is a terminal.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PrintNl()
  UartWrite(13)
  UartWrite(10)
EndProcedure
